Let the video go on after a lost frame instead of freezing until a keyframe.

After a sequence gap the video channel requests a keyframe and drops every frame until one
arrives, asking again every 200 ms. From a Steam Deck on Wi-Fi to a Samsung Q74T (Tizen 5.5,
TV on Ethernet), a 1080p keyframe at 30 Mbit/s spans hundreds of packets and often lost one
itself, so it was discarded too and the wait started over: the picture froze for 10 to 50 s
at a time, with dozens of requests and no complete keyframe in between.

With IHS_SessionConfig.keepPlayingAfterLoss set, a gap (or partial frames that stall) reports
the loss to the host, at most every 200 ms, and the following frames go on to the decoder. The
picture may smear until the host's keyframe lands, but it keeps moving. It is off by default,
which keeps the old behaviour; a decoder reset requested by the app still waits for a keyframe.

